{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2368 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "bad66d860c549a63c1c7a22321481b96464d77be8c6a81b7b0a427e849bef02e" :edb/db-before-t 2367 :edb/db-after-t 2368 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535680 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:01:09.235Z" 13194139535680 true] [17592186360811 1100 "Evaluate $\\lim_{z\\to1,\\ z\\ne1}f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=x^3y^2z^2-\\frac{3}{\\ln z}$, defined on $z>0$, $z\\ne1$." 13194139535680 true] [17592186360811 1100 "Evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1)$ for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.$" 13194139535680 false] [17592186360811 1145 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ at $(-1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1) & =12(-1)^{2}(2)(1)=24\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535680 false] [17592186360811 1145 "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xyz}(x,y,z)$ at $(-1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyz}(-1,2,1) & =12(-1)^{2}(2)(1)=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDomain clarification: the original function is undefined at $z=1$, since $\\ln1=0$. Consequently the literal value $f_{xyz}(-1,2,1)$ in the source prompt is undefined. The derivatives above are valid on $z>0$, $z\\ne1$; there $f_x=3x^2y^2z^2$, $f_{xy}=6x^2yz^2$, and $f_{xyz}=12x^2yz$. Thus $f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)=24z$ and\n$$\\lim_{z\\to1,\\ z\\ne1}f_{xyz}(-1,2,z)=24.$$\nThe clarified study question explicitly asks for this limit, or continuous extension of the derivative, so the source's accepted answer $24$ and all original choices remain valid. This extension does not define the original function or its literal derivative at the excluded point." 13194139535680 true]] :edb/tempids {}}
